INT. ABANDONED PLANETARIUM - NIGHT

A domed theater gone to ruin. Rows of broken chairs fan toward a hulking star projector. Moonlight slips through a crack in the dome. Dust drifts like constellations.

ALOYSIUS (60s, caretaker's coat, keys on his belt) leads the way with a flashlight. BRYONY (30s, journalist, notebook) follows. CASSIUS (20s, her photographer, three cameras round his neck) hangs back. DELPHINE (40s, astronomer, a thick scarf) runs a hand along the projector. EZRA (teen, hoodie, headphones, the caretaker's grandson) sits in the front row with his feet on the seat in front.

ALOYSIUS
The last show was in eighty-nine. They forgot to turn the stars off.

BRYONY
Forgot?

ALOYSIUS
Or didn't want to.

CASSIUS
I can't see anything.

DELPHINE
That's the point.

She flips a switch on the projector. Nothing. Another. A hum builds. The dome shudders. A smear of light climbs the plaster.

EZRA
(without looking up)
It's just the old bulb.

ALOYSIUS
It's the stars.

The light resolves into a tangle of pinpoints, swirling in the wrong places. Delphine tilts her head, frowning. The constellations are wrong. Orion is facing the wrong way, and a seventh star burns where there should be six.

BRYONY
Is that supposed to happen?

DELPHINE
No.

The seventh star pulses. Cassius lifts a camera and the flash blinds them. When their eyes recover, Ezra is standing in the aisle, staring up, headphones around his neck, mouth slightly open.

ALOYSIUS
Ezra.

EZRA
It knows my name.

ALOYSIUS
What did you say?

EZRA
It's whispering.

Delphine rushes to the projector and kills the power. The dome goes black. A single hiss ripples through the room, and then nothing. In the dark, Cassius's camera clicks once on its own.
